  { id:'opmcq-c6-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 19-year-old student presents every spring with recurrent itchy, watery eyes and mild papillary reaction on the tarsal conjunctiva. Symptoms are chronic and return each year. What is the best medication to prevent future episodes?",
    options:["Topical antibiotic","Mast cell stabilizer","Topical steroid","Oral antiviral"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The word doing the work is *prevent*.** The stem describes seasonal allergic conjunctivitis — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.87 prints its signs as exactly two lines, and the stem quotes the second of them:\n\n> **Signs:** – Conjunctival injection – **Mild papillary reaction.**\n\nand its symptoms as \"Itching, lacrimation, and red eye\". The same page prints the treatment list, and the question is which member of that list is **prophylactic** rather than symptomatic:\n\n> o Cold compresses to relieve itching.\n> o Artificial tears (to dilute alergens) *[sic — \"alergens\"]*\n> o **Topical mast cell stabilizers and antihistamines.**\n> o Topical steroids (although effective but are rarely needed)\n\n**Mast cell stabilizers and antihistamines are printed on one line, and only one of the two prevents.** An antihistamine blocks receptors that mediators have already reached, so it treats an attack that is under way. A mast cell stabiliser prevents degranulation in the first place, so it has to be started **before** the season and taken through it. The stem's \"prevent future episodes\" in a patient whose disease \"return[s] each year\" selects the stabiliser. *(The receptor-versus-degranulation mechanism is **not taken from the course material** — the book names both drug classes and explains neither.)*\n\n| Option | Where the course puts it | Why not here |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Topical antibiotic** | Bacterial conjunctivitis, p.91 | Nothing in the stem is infective — no discharge, no matting, no purulence. Itching is the allergic symptom |\n| **B. Mast cell stabilizer** | p.87 seasonal treatment, p.89 VKC treatment | The key |\n| **C. Topical steroid** | p.87, \"although effective but are **rarely needed**\" | ⚠️ The book's own parenthesis argues against it. Steroids abort an attack; they are not a prophylaxis, and p.89 warns to \"avoid long-term use\" — which is what a yearly-recurring disease would demand |\n| **D. Oral antiviral** | Nowhere in chapter 6 | No antiviral, oral or topical, is named anywhere in the chapter. Even for adenovirus the book prints \"no specific treatment necessary\" (p.90) |\n\n**C is the option worth arguing with rather than dismissing.** Topical steroid genuinely is effective in allergic conjunctivitis and the book says so. It fails on the stem's verb: a drug you give during a flare is not a drug that prevents future episodes, and this patient would need it every spring for years. The book's phrase \"rarely needed\" is the whole objection.\n\n⚠️ **The lecture cannot answer this.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` runs 66 slides for about 343 words and names no drug of any kind. Measured directly: `tacrolimus`, `cyclosporin`, `antihistamin` and `olopatadine` return **zero live hits** in that file — the only hits are inside the deck's own not-covered register at line 1149. This is a book answer.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain topic 6 keys the *diagnosis* of allergic disease repeatedly (`opqb-t6-224`, `opqb-t6-280`) and the *exclusion* of a treatment (`opqb-t6-266`, \"Vernal conjunctivitis is not treated with:\"), but no Grade Gain question asks for the preventive drug in seasonal disease.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Seasonal allergic conjunctivitis is itching plus a mild papillary reaction; of the drugs the book lists for it, the mast cell stabiliser is the one that prevents rather than treats, and the steroid is 'rarely needed'.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.43 (Ch.6 Q1)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 30-year-old woman complains of acute eye redness, burning and muco-purulent discharge. Upon examination, it was evident that she had conjunctival injection and chemosis and eyelid edema. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Viral conjunctivitis","Bacterial conjunctivitis","Vernal keratoconjunctivitis","Trachomatous conjunctivitis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The discharge decides it, and the book decides by discharge.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.85 prints a four-column table whose only job is to map discharge character onto cause:\n\n| Watery | Mucoid | Mucopurulent | Purulent |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Viral · Allergic | Allergic · Toxic & Dry eye | Chlamydial · **Acute bacterial infection** | **Acute bacterial infection** |\n\nMucopurulent belongs to bacterial and chlamydial disease and to nothing else. The rest of the stem then separates those two. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.91 gives acute bacterial conjunctivitis:\n\n> Symptoms: \"**Acute onset** of redness, grittiness, **burning** and discharge\"\n> Signs: \"**Oedema and erythema of the eyelids** · **Conjunctival injection and chemosis** · **Mucopurulent or purulent discharge**\"\n\nEvery clinical word in this stem — acute, burning, mucopurulent, injection, chemosis, lid oedema — is on that list, in that order. Chlamydial disease is excluded by its own printed tempo: p.91 makes adult chlamydial conjunctivitis a **subacute** onset with **tender preauricular nodes** and **follicles**, none of which the stem gives.\n\n| Option | The book's discriminating feature | Present here? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Viral** | **Watery** discharge, **follicles**, preauricular lymphadenopathy (pp.85, 90) | No — the discharge is wrong and no follicles are described |\n| **B. Bacterial** | Mucopurulent/purulent, lid oedema, chemosis, acute onset (p.91) | The key |\n| **C. VKC** | **Intense itching**, **ropy mucoid** discharge, giant papillae (pp.88, 94) | ⚠️ The stem never mentions itching at all, and that omission is deliberate |\n| **D. Trachomatous** | Upper tarsal **follicles**, chronic, preschool children, endemic setting (p.92) | No follicles, and the tempo is acute |\n\n**The DD grid on p.94 is the shortest version of this whole question**, and it is worth memorising as printed: itching mild/mild/**intense**, discharge watery/**mucopurulent-purulent**/mucoid-ropy, signs follicles+lymphadenopathy / **papillae** / cobblestone papillae.\n\n⚠️ **This stem is the antecedent of `opmcq-c6-3`**, which asks about its treatment. Read them together.\n\n⚠️ **What the bank does not tell you, and neither does the book.** No organism is named in this stem, and `ophthalmology.pdf` never names a topical antibiotic anywhere in chapter 6 — \"Topical antibiotics\" is as specific as it gets. The lecture is worse: `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` names **no organism at all** in 66 slides. So this question can be answered from the course, but the follow-up \"with what?\" cannot be.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-250` (a 5-year-old with acute redness, mucopurulent discharge, conjunctival hyperaemia and mild lid oedema) is the same disease with the same sign list in a child; `opqb-t6-270` (lids stuck together on waking) keys the same diagnosis through the book's *matting* symptom, which this stem omits.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Mucopurulent discharge with acute onset, chemosis and lid oedema and no itching is acute bacterial conjunctivitis; watery plus follicles is viral, ropy plus intense itching is vernal, and chlamydial disease is subacute with follicles.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.43 (Ch.6 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 30-year-old woman has acute bacterial conjunctivitis — acute redness, burning, muco-purulent discharge, conjunctival injection, chemosis and eyelid edema. Which of the following is true regarding treatment?",
    options:["Eye patching is contraindicated","Strict hygiene is mandatory","Topical antibiotics are usually sufficient to eradicate the infection in most cases","All of the above"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, which of the following is true regarding treatment?\"** The antecedent is `opmcq-c6-2` — the 30-year-old woman with mucopurulent discharge — one question back on the same book page, and it has been read rather than assumed. The stem above restates her, and it names the diagnosis because `opmcq-c6-2` has already keyed it; withholding it here would make this a second diagnosis question rather than the treatment question the bank wrote.\n\n**All three statements are printed, in one four-line block.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.91, treatment of acute bacterial conjunctivitis:\n\n> • **Avoid eye patching** and stop the use of contact lenses by the patient.\n> • **Strict hygienic measures**\n> • **Topical antibiotics**\n> • **Systemic** antibiotics are needed in gonococcal and meningococcal and H influenzae infections.\n\nThe bank has taken the first three bullets, turned each into a sentence, and added \"All of the above\". So **D**.\n\n| Option | The book's line | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Eye patching is contraindicated** | \"Avoid eye patching\" | True. A patch turns the closed sac into a warm, moist, undrained culture space, and it removes the blink that clears discharge *(the mechanism is **not taken from the course material**; the book gives the instruction without the reason)* |\n| **B. Strict hygiene is mandatory** | \"Strict hygienic measures\" | True. The disease is \"caused by direct contact with infected secretions\" (p.91), so hygiene is transmission control, not comfort |\n| **C. Topical antibiotics are usually sufficient in most cases** | \"Topical antibiotics\" as the routine line, with systemic reserved for gonococcal, meningococcal and H. influenzae | True **for most cases** — and the qualifier is what makes it true. The three exceptions are named on the next line |\n| **D. All of the above** | — | The key |\n\n**Option C is the one a careful reader hesitates over, and the hesitation is the point.** The book does list situations needing systemic antibiotics, so \"topical is sufficient\" is not universally true. But the option says *usually* and *in most cases*, and the exceptions are three named organisms out of a disease whose commonest causes are \"H. influenzae, S. pneumoniae, S. aureus and Moraxella catarrhalis\" (p.91). ⚠️ Note the awkwardness the book creates: **H. influenzae is both a common cause and a systemic-antibiotic indication.** The bank does not test that overlap here, but it is on the page.\n\n⚠️ **Neither the book nor the deck names a single topical antibiotic.** Chapter 6's own not-covered register records it: \"Any named topical antibiotic anywhere in the chapter: absent.\" So \"topical antibiotics\" is the most specific answer the course permits, which is precisely why the bank could write option C as a class and not a drug.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-227`, \"**Patching** of the eye is **contraindicated** in:\", asks option A from the opposite direction and is the strongest single-fact match in the chapter; `opqb-t6-238`, \"In treatment of **mucopurulent conjunctivitis**, all are true **EXCEPT**:\", covers the same four-bullet block as a negative-stem question.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The book's four-bullet treatment of acute bacterial conjunctivitis is avoid patching, strict hygiene, topical antibiotics, and systemic antibiotics only for gonococcal, meningococcal and H. influenzae disease — so the first three are all true together.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.43 (Ch.6 Q3; the stem is printed "In the previous case, which of the following is true regarding treatment?" and refers back to Ch.6 Q2)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"Which of the following viruses is the most common cause of viral conjunctivitis?",
    options:["Herpes simplex virus","Adenovirus","Epstein-Barr virus","Mumps virus"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Straight recall, and the book prints the figure.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.90 opens its viral-conjunctivitis section with three bullets, the second of which is the whole answer:\n\n> • The **most common external ocular infection**\n> • **Adenovirus** is the most frequent causative agent (**90% of cases**)\n> • It is highly contagious and may appear as epidemics.\n\n**Ninety per cent** is the number to carry; it is the only frequency figure the chapter gives for any organism.\n\n| Option | Where the course puts it | Why not the key |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Herpes simplex** | p.90, in the clinical-presentation table: \"Herpes simplex virus follicular conjunctivitis (blepharoconjunctivitis)\" | Real and named, but it is one row of a five-row table in which adenovirus occupies three |\n| **B. Adenovirus** | p.90, 90 % of cases | The key |\n| **C. Epstein-Barr virus** | **Nowhere in chapter 6** | Not named once in printed 82–95 |\n| **D. Mumps virus** | p.90, last row: \"Systemic viral infections e.g., varicella, measles, and **mumps**\" | Named, but as a systemic infection with ocular involvement, not as a cause of conjunctivitis in its own right |\n\n**The serovar split is the fact this question is a gateway to**, and it is examinable in its own right:\n\n| Adenoviral syndrome | Serovars | The book's phrase |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pharyngoconjunctival fever | **3, 4 and 7** | — |\n| Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis | **8, 19 and 37** | \"The **most severe** ocular adenoviral infection\" |\n| Non-specific acute follicular conjunctivitis | not specified | \"The **most common** form\" |\n\n⚠️ **A second source names the same serovars and disagrees slightly.** `L37) Red Eye.txt` — which is a book chapter, not a slide deck — prints at line 490 that upper respiratory infection with fever \"may be associated with conjunctivitis, particularly when these symptoms are due to **adenovirus type 3 or type 7** (both of which cause pharyngoconjunctival fever)\". It gives 3 and 7 where `ophthalmology.pdf` gives 3, 4 and 7. Both are recorded; neither is corrected, and the key is untouched.\n\n⚠️ **The lecture deck names no virus at all.** Measured: `adenovirus` returns **zero live hits** in `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` — its single hit is inside the deck's own not-covered register at line 1116, alongside `Corynebact = 0` and `\"Chlamydia trachomatis\" = 0`. Slides 34–36 are the viral-conjunctivitis section and they are photographs under a title.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-251` (\"Concerning **adenoviral conjunctivitis**:\") and `opqb-t6-229` (\"In **viral epidemic kerato-conjunctivitis** characteristically there is usually:\") both work the same book page; neither asks for the frequency, so this recall point is House's alone.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Adenovirus causes 90 % of viral conjunctivitis; serovars 3, 4 and 7 give pharyngoconjunctival fever and 8, 19 and 37 give epidemic keratoconjunctivitis, the most severe form.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.43 (Ch.6 Q4)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"What is the recurrence rate of pterygium following excision (with conjunctival autograft)?",
    options:["5%","10%","15%","50%"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**A bare number, and the book prints it in a parenthesis.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.95, under pterygium management:\n\n> - **One-third recur after excision**, lower recurrence with conjunctival *autograft* (**only 5% recurrence rate**), or *mitomycin-c* after simple excision.\n\nThat single line carries **two** numbers, and the question turns on reading which belongs to which technique:\n\n| Technique | Recurrence | The book's words |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Simple excision with bare sclera** | **one-third (~33 %)** | \"One-third recur after excision\" |\n| Excision **+ conjunctival autograft** | **5 %** | \"only 5% recurrence rate\" |\n| Excision + mitomycin-C | lower than bare sclera; **no figure given** | named as an alternative, unquantified |\n\n**Option D, 50 %, is the designed trap** — it is close enough to one-third that a reader who has retained \"a lot of pterygia come back\" but not which operation will reach for it. Options B and C are filler; neither figure appears anywhere in chapter 6.\n\n**Why the graft works is figure-only teaching**, and worth knowing because the book hides it. The uncaptioned line diagram on p.95 shows an eye labelled *Pterygium in medial limbus*, an arrow to a *Harvested area* at the **superior bulbar conjunctiva**, and a *Grafted area* at the bare nasal site. The prose names the autograft and its 5 % figure but **never says where the graft comes from** — only the diagram does. The book's chapter cache flags this explicitly as figure-only teaching.\n\n⚠️ **The bare-sclera technique is the one the book names for the operation itself**: \"Simple excision with bare sclera\" is the printed heading, with four printed indications — chronic inflammation, threat to the visual axis, induced astigmatism, cosmesis. A question asking *when* to operate is answered from that list; this question asks what happens afterwards.\n\n⚠️ **Two other sources cover pterygium and neither gives a recurrence figure.** `L37) Red Eye.txt` line 268 describes it as \"a triangular fold of tissue that advances progressively over the cornea, usually from the nasal side\", associates it with ultraviolet exposure and tropical climates, and says only that \"Surgical excision is indicated if the pterygium starts to encroach on the visual axis\". `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` gives pterygium **eight slides** (50–57), including an operative photograph of excision with a sutured graft on slide 57 — and not one word of text beyond the title. Measured: no number appears anywhere in those eight blocks.\n\n**Cross-bank:** Grade Gain topic 6 asks pterygium four times — `opqb-t6-264` (\"**Pterygium** can be a cause of the following **except**\"), `opqb-t6-267` (\"**Pterygium** is caused by the following factor\"), `opqb-t6-281` (a 24-year-old soldier with a nasal triangular membrane just beyond the limbus) and `opqb-t6-284` (bilateral yellow triangular masses, management) — and **none of the four asks a recurrence rate**. Checked across all 63 topic-6 stems.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Pterygium recurs in about one-third after simple bare-sclera excision but in only 5 % when a conjunctival autograft is used; the graft is harvested from the superior bulbar conjunctiva, which the book teaches only in a diagram.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.44 (Ch.6 Q5)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding pingueculum except?",
    options:["It usually arises in those not exposed adequately to sun light","It usually accompanies aging","It usually causes mild irritative manifestations","It is usually removed surgically only for cosmesis"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints the keyed option as \"not exposed adquately to sun light\"** — *adquately* for *adequately*. Repaired above; the misprint is recorded in the staging file and does not touch the meaning, because the option is false either way.\n\n⚠️ **\"Pingueculum\" is NOT a misprint and is not corrected.** It is the spelling the course uses. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.94 heads the section \"**1. Pingueculum**\" and captions \"**Fig 6-7: Pingueculum**\", and `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` slide 58 is titled *Pingueculum* with the transcriber's note that this is \"the lecturer's spelling ... not the usual 'pinguecula'\". ⚠️ The book then contradicts itself in a caption on the same page — the other figure is captioned \"**Pinguecula** (ppt)\". Both spellings are the book's own, recorded and not corrected.\n\n**Three of the four options are p.94 bullets; the fourth is a p.94 bullet turned inside out.** The whole entry reads:\n\n> ▪ **Yellow white subepithelial** deposit of hyaline and elastic tissue adjacent to the nasal or temporal limbus, *sparing the cornea*\n> ▪ Associated with **sun and wind exposure, aging**\n> ▪ Benign, sometimes enlarges slowly\n> ▪ May be **irritating** due to abnormal tear film distribution\n> ▪ **Treatment:** Irritative symptoms may be treated with **lubricating drops** · Surgical excision for **cosmesis only**\n\n| Option | The book's line | True? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Arises in those NOT exposed adequately to sunlight** | \"Associated with **sun** and wind exposure\" | ⚠️ **FALSE — and it is an exact inversion.** The key |\n| **B. Usually accompanies aging** | \"Associated with sun and wind exposure, **aging**\" | True, same bullet |\n| **C. Usually causes mild irritative manifestations** | \"May be **irritating** due to abnormal tear film distribution\" | True. The mechanism is mechanical — the raised mound breaks the tear film over it |\n| **D. Usually removed surgically only for cosmesis** | \"Surgical excision for **cosmesis only**\" | True, verbatim |\n\n**The trap is that A reads like a plausible sentence.** It has the right shape — an environmental association, hedged with *usually* — and only the polarity is wrong. This is the commonest form of an *except* question in this bank: three verbatim lifts and one lift with a negation inserted.\n\n**The pterygium contrast is the fact behind the fact**, and the book teaches it with an Arabic marginal gloss beside the pterygium heading on p.95 — تيجي على الكورنيا, \"it comes onto the cornea\":\n\n| | Pingueculum | Pterygium |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cornea** | ***Sparing* the cornea** | ***Encroachment* onto the cornea** |\n| Shape | Yellow-white subepithelial deposit | Fibrovascular, triangular, wing-like |\n| Vision | Not affected | May reduce it — induced astigmatism or covering the pupillary area |\n| Surgery | **Cosmesis only** | Four indications: chronic inflammation, threat to the visual axis, induced astigmatism, cosmesis |\n\n⚠️ **A third source prints the same discriminator.** `L15,16) Cornea.txt` lines 352–357 set them side by side: \"Pterygium: fibrovascular growth from conjunctiva\" against \"Pinguecula: yellowish nasal/temporal conjunctival lesion; does NOT cross limbus (cf. pterygium)\". That deck spells it *Pinguecula*; `L6` and the book heading spell it *Pingueculum*. **Three spellings of one lesion across the module's own sources.**\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-284`, \"A male patient complains of **bilateral small yellow triangular masses on either side of the cornea**. Your decision for management should be:\", keys the same cosmesis-only rule from a vignette instead of a list.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Pingueculum is associated WITH sun and wind exposure and ageing, is irritating through tear-film breakup, and is excised for cosmesis only — and unlike pterygium it spares the cornea.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.44 (Ch.6 Q6; option A is printed "not exposed adquately to sun light")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 9-year-old child complains of bilateral watery eye discharge accompanied by eye discharge. On examination, it was evident that he had pre-auricular lymphadenopathy. Which of the following is the most probable diagnosis?",
    options:["Atopic keratoconjunctivitis","Spring catarrh","Adenoviral keratoconjunctivitis","None of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The stem is redundant as printed** — *\"bilateral watery eye discharge accompanied by eye discharge\"*. It is transcribed verbatim above and **not repaired**, because there is no way to recover what the second clause was meant to say. A guess would be an invention, and an invented clause in a stem can leak or block the answer. What survives the redundancy is *bilateral* and *watery*, and those two words plus the node are enough.\n\n**Two findings, one disease.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.85 puts **watery** discharge under **Viral** and **Allergic**; p.86 puts **preauricular lymphadenopathy** under **Viral**, **Chlamydial** and **severe bacterial (especially gonococcal)** — and *not* under allergic. Only **viral** appears on both lists, and p.90 makes adenovirus 90 % of viral conjunctivitis. So **C**.\n\n| Option | Discharge | Preauricular node? | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Atopic keratoconjunctivitis** | \"More watery than in VKC\" (p.89) | **No** — allergic disease is not on the p.86 node list | ⚠️ The discharge fits and the node does not. AKC is also \"a rare bilateral condition that affects **adults (30-50 years)**\" and \"worse in the **winter**\" (p.89) — this patient is 9 |\n| **B. Spring catarrh (VKC)** | **Mucous \"ropy\"** (p.88) | No | The discharge is wrong, and the stem's cardinal VKC symptom — *severe itching*, the book's \"morning misery\" — is absent |\n| **C. Adenoviral keratoconjunctivitis** | **Watery** (p.85) | **Yes** (p.86, p.90) | The key |\n| **D. None of the above** | — | — | Fails because C is correct |\n\n**A is the option that has to be argued away rather than dismissed**, and the argument is the lymph node. Atopic keratoconjunctivitis is genuinely more watery than VKC — the book says so in those words — so a reader anchoring only on \"watery\" can land there. The preauricular node is what forbids it: allergic conjunctivitis of every type is absent from the p.86 lymphadenopathy list, and this bank tests that same list head-on in `opmcq-c6-18`. The age is the second objection.\n\n**What the bank left out, and it matters.** The book gives adenoviral disease **follicles** \"mainly in the lower tarsal conjunctiva\" (p.90) as its characteristic sign, and the stem never mentions the tarsal conjunctiva at all. The question is therefore answerable on discharge + node alone, which is a thinner basis than the book's own picture. It works because no other option can hold both findings.\n\n⚠️ **The epidemic context is the classic exam framing and this stem omits it.** The book prints that adenoviral disease \"is highly contagious and **may appear as epidemics**\" and is \"transmitted by contact with respiratory or ocular secretions\" (p.90); `L37) Red Eye.txt` line 490 adds the upper-respiratory-infection-with-fever association. A single child with no contact history is a harder version of the same question.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-273` gives the epidemic version — \"In August 1999 many Egyptian citizens complained of bilateral red watery eyes... a marked **follicular reaction**\" — and `opqb-t6-226` gives the pharyngoconjunctival-fever version, a child with fever and sore throat and follicles in the **lower** palpebral conjunctiva. Both are the same organism reached by a different route; neither shares this stem.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Watery discharge appears under viral and allergic disease, but preauricular lymphadenopathy appears under viral, chlamydial and severe bacterial and never under allergic — so watery plus a node is adenoviral.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.44 (Ch.6 Q7; the stem is printed "bilateral watery eye discharge accompanied by eye discharge")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"According to the WHO trachoma grading, which of the following is meant by TT?",
    options:["5 or more follicles on the superior tarsal plate","Diffuse involvement of tarsal conjunctiva","Easily visible fibrous white tarsal bands","At least one lash touching the globe","Corneal opacification"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The five options are the five WHO grades, in the WHO's own order, and the stem picks one.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.93 prints the simplified system in a rounded box:\n\n| Grade | Stands for | The book's definition | Option |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **TF** | Trachomatous inflammation (**follicular**) | \"**5 or more follicles** [**more than 0.5 mm in diameter**] on the superior tarsal plate\" | A |\n| **TI** | Trachomatous inflammation (**intense**) | \"Diffuse involvement of the tarsal conjunctiva, obscuring **50 % or more** of the deep tarsal vessels. Papillae are present\" | B |\n| **TS** | Trachomatous conjunctival **scarring** | \"**Easily** visible fibrous white tarsal bands\" | C |\n| **TT** | Trachomatous **trichiasis** | \"**At least one lash** touching the globe\" | **D — the key** |\n| **CO** | **Corneal opacity** | \"Sufficient to blur details of at least part of the pupillary margin\" | E |\n\n**TT is trichiasis**, and the mnemonic is the second T. The book's threshold is deliberately minimal — *at least one lash* — because the grade exists to trigger surgery, which is the **S** of the SAFE strategy on the same page.\n\n⚠️ **THIS QUESTION IS PAIRED WITH `opmcq-c6-23`.** The two print an **identical five-option menu** — same wording, same order, same misprint — and differ only in the two-letter grade the stem names: **TT** here, **TS** there. Verified character by character at 2.2× zoom on both book pages. Answering one and misreading the other's stem is the designed failure, and the two sit sixteen questions apart so the pairing is invisible in a linear read.\n\n⚠️ **Option C is misprinted in both, and repaired in both.** The bank prints \"**Early** visible fibrous white tarsal bands\"; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.93 prints \"**Easily** visible fibrous white tarsal bands\". It is not the key here, so this answer is unaffected either way — but it *is* the key in `opmcq-c6-23`, and the two menus are identical, so the repair is applied to both rather than to the keyed one alone.\n\n**Two numbers the bank did not ask for but that live in the same box**, and both are droppable in a short-answer paper: TF needs **five** follicles of **more than 0.5 mm**, and TI needs **50 % or more** of the deep tarsal vessels obscured. Neither threshold is in any option here.\n\n⚠️ **The WHO system is book-only, and the older system is taught nowhere.** Measured across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks: `MacCallan` returns **zero live hits** — its single hit is inside `L6) Conjunctiva.txt`'s own not-covered register at line 1104, which records \"MacCallan = 0 hits (also spelt McCallan = 0)\". `\"SAFE strategy\"`, `\"face washing\"` and `notifi*` likewise return zero live hits in that deck (line 1170). `L6` gives trachoma nine slides (25–33) — follicles, pannus, nebula ex pannus, PTCs, scarring — and **not one grade letter**.\n\n**Cross-bank: Grade Gain topic 6 asks the WHO grading NOWHERE.** Checked across all 63 stems, `opqb-t6-224` to `opqb-t6-288`. Its trachoma questions are activity questions (`opqb-t6-233` \"In **trachoma** the patient is **infectious** when there is:\", `opqb-t6-246` \"**Signs of activity** of trachoma include:\"), complication questions (`opqb-t6-242`, `opqb-t6-274`) and sign questions (`opqb-t6-230`, `opqb-t6-285`). The grading letters are House's alone.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"WHO simplified trachoma grading is TF five or more follicles over 0.5 mm on the superior tarsus, TI diffuse involvement obscuring half the deep vessels, TS easily visible white tarsal bands, TT at least one lash touching the globe, CO corneal opacity blurring the pupillary margin.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.44 (Ch.6 Q8; option C is printed "Early visible fibrous white tarsal bands", where ophthalmology.pdf p.93 prints "Easily visible")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"All of the following are among the characteristics of active trachoma except?",
    options:["Upper tarsal follicular conjunctivitis with mucopurulent discharge","Superior epithelial keratitis with pannus formation","Herbert pits","Papillary reaction"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book splits trachoma into two stages in one table, and this question tests the seam.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.92:\n\n> **Active trachoma** — \"most common in **preschool children**\":\n> o **Upper tarsal follicular conjunctivitis associated with mucopurulent discharge.**\n> o **Papillary reaction** can also be present\n> o **Cornea:** ▪ **Superior epithelial keratitis** ▪ **pannus** formation (superficial corneal vascularization and cellular infiltration)\n>\n> **Cicatricial trachoma** — \"prevalent in **middle age**\":\n> ... **Herbert pits:** Superior limbal follicles may resolve to leave a row of shallow depressions\n\nOptions A, B and D are the three active bullets, near-verbatim. **C is lifted out of the cicatricial row of the same table.** So **C**.\n\n| Option | Stage | The book's phrase |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A** | Active | \"Upper tarsal follicular conjunctivitis associated with mucopurulent discharge\" |\n| **B** | Active | \"Superior epithelial keratitis\" + \"pannus formation\" |\n| **C. Herbert pits** | **Cicatricial** | \"Superior limbal follicles may **resolve** to leave a row of shallow depressions\" — the key |\n| **D** | Active | \"Papillary reaction can also be present\" |\n\n**The word that settles C is *resolve*.** A Herbert pit is not an active lesion at all; it is the **scar left behind** by a limbal follicle that has healed. That makes it, like Arlt's line and the post-trachomatous concretions, a marker of past disease — which is why it belongs to the cicatricial stage and why it is the answer here.\n\n⚠️ **THIS QUESTION IS THE MIRROR OF `opmcq-c6-14`**, which asks the cicatricial half and keys **pannus** — the very line that is option B here. The two are built from the same table read in opposite directions, and they sit five questions apart. If you can place every row of the p.92 table on the correct side, both fall.\n\n**The two stages have considerable overlap and the book says so** — \"with considerable overlap\" is its own phrase — which is why a grading system exists at all. Do not read the split as chronologically clean.\n\n⚠️ **Herbert pits are photographed in the book and mislabelled in the photograph.** The p.92 limbal close-up carries black arrows and the in-figure label **\"Herber't Pit\"** — the apostrophe in the wrong place — while the body text prints \"Herbert pits\". Recorded, not corrected.\n\n⚠️ **The eponym is taught nowhere in the lectures.** Measured case-insensitively across all 29 cached decks: `Herbert` returns hits in exactly two files, and **both are absence registers** — `L5) Eyelid disease.txt` line 1295 (\"ABSENT: Hutchinson's sign, Herbert's pits, Arlt's line — zero each\") and `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` line 1126 (\"Herbert (Herbert's pits) = 0 · Arlt (Arlt's line) = 0\"). There is **no live hit anywhere in the module's decks.** `L6`'s trachoma section photographs the signs and names none of them.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-230` asks \"Corneal **Herbert's rosettes** are found in:\" — ⚠️ **Grade Gain calls them ROSETTES where House and the book call them PITS.** Same lesion, third name. `opqb-t6-246` (\"**Signs of activity** of trachoma include:\") is the direct positive-stem twin of this question; `opqb-t6-261` and `opqb-t6-285` work the same table.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Active trachoma is upper tarsal follicles with mucopurulent discharge, a papillary reaction, superior epithelial keratitis and pannus; Herbert pits are the scars left when limbal follicles resolve, so they belong to the cicatricial stage.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.44 (Ch.6 Q9)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 23-year-old male patient presents with a complaint of red eyes, itching and excessive lacrimation especially during spring and summer seasons. Examination revealed giant tarsal papillae, punctate epithelial corneal erosions and a mild degree of keratoconus. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Seasonal allergic conjunctivitis","Vernal keratoconjunctivitis","Atopic keratoconjunctivitis","Viral conjunctivitis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints \"presnets\"** for *presents*. Repaired above; recorded in the staging file. ⚠️ **The same typo and about thirty words of this stem reappear in `opmcq-c6-26`** on book p.48 — one template used twice inside this chapter, with the age changed and the question turned from diagnosis to additional-finding.\n\n**Three signs, and the book gives all three to one disease.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.88 divides VKC's signs into conjunctival, limbal and corneal:\n\n> **Conjunctival signs:** Diffuse conjunctival injection · Tarsal **giant papillae**\n> **Corneal signs** (p.89): **Punctate epithelial erosions** · **Shield ulcer** · **Pseudo-gerontoxon** · \"**Keratoconus** is more common in VKC patients as well; possibly due to increased eye rubbing of chronically irritated patients\"\n\nGiant papillae, punctate epithelial erosions and keratoconus are three consecutive entries from that list. Add the printed symptoms — \"Severe itching · Photophobia · Foreign body sensation · Mucous 'ropy' discharge\" — and the seasonal recurrence the disease is named for (*vernal* = springtime), and every clause in the stem is on pp.88–89.\n\n| Option | Age the book gives | Season | The sign that excludes it |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Seasonal allergic** | not specified | \"worse during spring and summer\" | ⚠️ **Signs are \"Conjunctival injection\" and \"Mild papillary reaction\" only** (p.87). Seasonal disease does **not** give giant papillae, corneal erosions or keratoconus — it has no corneal involvement at all |\n| **B. VKC** | **5–25**, onset 10–12 | vernal, 23 % perennial | The key |\n| **C. AKC** | **adults 30–50** | \"worse in the **winter**\" | Wrong age, wrong season, and its papillae are **small** (p.89), not giant |\n| **D. Viral** | — | — | Watery discharge and **follicles**, not papillae; and itching in viral disease is \"Mild\" on the p.94 grid |\n\n**A is the trap, and the discriminator is corneal involvement.** Seasonal allergic conjunctivitis and VKC share the season, the itch and the allergic mechanism. What separates them is that VKC reaches the cornea and seasonal disease does not — and the bank makes exactly that contrast explicit in `opmcq-c6-12`, which prints the **same four options** with the signs downgraded to \"mild papillary reaction coupled with conjunctival injection\" and keys **A**. Read the two together; they are a designed pair.\n\n**Why keratoconus is on the list is worth holding**, because it is the only sign here that is not inflammation: chronic itching drives chronic eye rubbing, and rubbing thins and deforms the cornea. The book gives that reasoning in a parenthesis and its treatment section opens on it — \"Instruct the patient to **avoid eye rubbing** as much as possible (result in mast cell degranulation and leads to a vicious circle of more itching and eye rubbing)\" (p.89).\n\n⚠️ **The age is a stretch and the bank knows it.** This patient is 23; the book puts VKC at 5–25 with onset 10–12 and \"spontaneous resolution in the **late second decade**\". Twenty-three is inside the range and past the usual resolution — consistent with the 23 % who run a perennial course. The signs, not the age, carry the diagnosis.\n\n⚠️ **This stem is the antecedent of `opmcq-c6-11`.**\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-224` (\"recurrent bilateral conjunctivitis occurring with the onset of **hot weather** in young boys\"), `opqb-t6-280` (a 10-year-old with extreme itching and ropy white discharge on lid eversion) and `opqb-t6-253` (\"All of the following are true regarding **Vernal keratoconjunctivitis EXCEPT**\") are the same disease; none shares this stem or its typo.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Giant tarsal papillae with punctate epithelial erosions and keratoconus in a seasonally itchy patient is vernal keratoconjunctivitis; seasonal allergic conjunctivitis gives only injection and a mild papillary reaction and never touches the cornea.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.45 (Ch.6 Q10; the stem is printed "presnets")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 23-year-old male patient has vernal keratoconjunctivitis — seasonal itching and lacrimation with giant tarsal papillae, punctate epithelial corneal erosions and mild keratoconus. What is the most appropriate line of management?",
    options:["Systemic steroids","Systemic cyclosporine","Topical anti-histamines","Antibiotics"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, what is the most appropriate line of management?\"** The antecedent is `opmcq-c6-10`, one question back on the same book page, and it has been read rather than assumed. The stem above restates that patient and names the diagnosis, because `opmcq-c6-10` has already keyed it.\n\n**Every drug in VKC is topical, and that single word answers the question.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.89 prints the treatment as one indented block under a heading that is itself the discriminator:\n\n> o Instruct the patient to **avoid eye rubbing** as much as possible\n> o Dark glasses and cold compresses.\n> o **Medications: → Topical:**\n>   ▪ mast cell stabilizers\n>   ▪ **antihistamines.**\n>   ▪ vasoconstrictor and anti-histaminic drops (during the attack only)\n>   ▪ steroid to control acute attacks but avoid long-term use\n>   ▪ cyclosporine or topical tacrolimus may be considered for **severe cases** and as steroid-sparing agents\n\nAntihistamines are on the list; the route printed above the list is **Topical**. Option C is the only option that matches both.\n\n| Option | Is the drug on the book's list? | Is the ROUTE the book's? | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Systemic steroids** | Steroid yes | **No — topical** | ⚠️ Route error. And the book restricts even the topical steroid to \"acute attacks\", warning to \"avoid long-term use\" |\n| **B. Systemic cyclosporine** | Cyclosporine yes | **No — topical** | ⚠️ Route error, and a severity error: cyclosporine is reserved for \"severe cases\" and as a steroid-sparing agent |\n| **C. Topical anti-histamines** | Yes | Yes | The key |\n| **D. Antibiotics** | **Not anywhere in VKC** | — | VKC is allergic, not infective. Antibiotics appear in this chapter only under bacterial, chlamydial and trachomatous disease |\n\n**Both A and B are real VKC drugs given by the wrong road, and that is the entire design of this menu.** A reader who has memorised the drug names without the route heading will take either. The book prints \"**Medications: → Topical:**\" as a single line above all five sub-bullets, so the route governs the whole list — including the cyclosporine that option B systematises.\n\n**Why topical is the right road here** is not stated by the book, so it is worth naming as outside knowledge: the target tissue is the ocular surface, a drop reaches it directly at high local concentration, and systemic antihistamines or steroids would expose the whole patient to achieve the same surface effect. *(**Not taken from the course material** — the book gives the route without a rationale.)*\n\n⚠️ **The first line of the book's treatment is not a drug at all.** \"Avoid eye rubbing\" heads the list, ahead of every medication, because rubbing degranulates mast cells and closes a vicious circle — and because it is what drives the keratoconus this patient already has. No option offers it, but in a written paper it is the first thing to write.\n\n⚠️ **The lecture names no drug.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` gives allergic disease and VKC nine slides (37–45), all photographs; `antihistamin`, `cyclosporin`, `tacrolimus` and `olopatadine` each return **zero live hits**, their only appearance being the deck's own not-covered register at line 1149.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-266`, \"**Vernal conjunctivitis** is **not** treated with:\", is the negative-stem twin and the closest match; `opqb-t6-225` (a boy on long-term spring-catarrh treatment who loses vision) tests the steroid warning this question's option A ignores.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Every VKC medication the book prints is topical — mast cell stabilisers, antihistamines, vasoconstrictors, short-course steroid, and cyclosporine or tacrolimus for severe cases — so a systemic route is wrong however familiar the drug.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.45 (Ch.6 Q11; the stem is printed "In the previous case, what is the most appropriate line of management?" and refers back to Ch.6 Q10)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 2-year-old child is brought by his parents to the ophthalmology clinic. They report that he suffers from red eye, itching and excessive lacrimation especially during spring and summer seasons. Examination revealed mild papillary reaction coupled with conjunctival injection. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Seasonal allergic conjunctivitis","Vernal keratoconjunctivitis","Atopic keratoconjunctivitis","Viral conjunctivitis"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**This is `opmcq-c6-10` with the signs taken away, and the option menu is identical to prove it.** Same four options, same order, same seasonal history — and the examination findings downgraded from *giant tarsal papillae, punctate epithelial erosions, keratoconus* to *mild papillary reaction and conjunctival injection*. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.87 prints seasonal allergic conjunctivitis's signs as exactly those two lines and no others:\n\n> **Symptoms:** – Itching, lacrimation, and red eye ( ± sneezing and nasal discharge)\n> **Signs:** – **Conjunctival injection** – **Mild papillary reaction.**\n\nThe stem quotes the signs box whole. So **A**.\n\n| Option | Age the book gives | The finding that decides it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Seasonal allergic** | not specified | \"Mild papillary reaction\" + \"Conjunctival injection\", and **no corneal signs at all** — the key |\n| **B. VKC** | **5–25 years**, onset 10–12 | ⚠️ Needs **giant** tarsal papillae; a *mild* papillary reaction is the wrong grade. And the child is **2** — below the book's range |\n| **C. AKC** | **adults 30–50 years** | Wrong age by three decades, and \"worse in the **winter**\" where this is spring and summer |\n| **D. Viral** | — | Viral disease gives **follicles** and **watery** discharge, and its itching is \"Mild\" on the p.94 grid; the stem's cardinal symptom is itching with a seasonal rhythm |\n\n**Two independent discriminators point the same way, and it is worth being able to state both.**\n\n1. **The grade of the papilla.** The book puts a number on it: giant papillae are \"those **more than 1mm in diameter**\" (p.86), and they belong to prolonged inflammation — VKC, prolonged contact lens wear, foreign body. A *mild* papillary reaction is not that.\n2. **The age.** VKC is 5–25 with onset 10–12; AKC is 30–50. A 2-year-old is outside both. Seasonal allergic conjunctivitis is the only allergic form the book gives no age limit to.\n\n⚠️ **B is genuinely tempting and the reason is the season.** Everything about the history — spring, summer, itching, watering, recurrence — is shared by seasonal disease and VKC, because they are the same hypersensitivity in two grades. What the bank withheld is the entire severity axis: no giant papillae, no ropy discharge, no photophobia, no corneal erosion, no shield ulcer. **A stem that omits every severe feature is telling you the disease is mild.**\n\n⚠️ **A caution the book supports.** VKC \"is a condition seen primarily in hot and dry climates most commonly in West Africa, the **Mediterranean basin, and the Middle East**\" (p.88), so in this course's own setting VKC is common and the reflex is strong. The age and the papilla grade are what must override it.\n\n⚠️ **This stem is the antecedent of `opmcq-c6-13`.** Checked for the *presnets* typo that runs through `opmcq-c6-10` and `opmcq-c6-26` — this stem is clean, and \"conjunctival injection\" is spelt correctly.\n\n**Cross-bank:** no Grade Gain topic-6 question keys **seasonal allergic conjunctivitis** by name — checked across all 63 stems. `opqb-t6-224` keys vernal from a near-identical seasonal history, which makes it the closest thing to a twin and a fold hazard worth flagging rather than a match.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A mild papillary reaction with conjunctival injection and no corneal sign, in a child below the vernal age range, is seasonal allergic conjunctivitis; giant papillae over 1 mm with corneal erosion would make it vernal.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.45 (Ch.6 Q12)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 2-year-old child has seasonal allergic conjunctivitis — red eye, itching and excessive lacrimation each spring and summer, with a mild papillary reaction and conjunctival injection. What is the most appropriate line of management?",
    options:["Systemic steroids","Local steroids","Topical anti-histamines","Antibiotics"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints this stem as \"In the previous case, what is the most appropriate line of management?\"** The antecedent is `opmcq-c6-12`, one question back on the same book page, and it has been read rather than assumed.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **THE OPTION MENU IS NOT THE ONE `opmcq-c6-11` USED, despite the two stems being word-for-word identical.** Option B reads **\"Local steroids\"** here where `opmcq-c6-11` read \"Systemic cyclosporine\". A shared stem is not a shared question — check the options before reusing an answer.\n\n**The book's four-line treatment of seasonal allergic conjunctivitis, `ophthalmology.pdf` p.87:**\n\n> o Cold compresses to relieve itching.\n> o Artificial tears (to dilute alergens) *[sic]*\n> o **Topical mast cell stabilizers and antihistamines.**\n> o Topical steroids (**although effective but are rarely needed**)\n\nOption C is on line three. So **C**.\n\n| Option | The book's position | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Systemic steroids** | Not offered anywhere for allergic conjunctivitis | Wrong drug and wrong route for a mild surface disease in a **2-year-old** |\n| **B. Local steroids** | \"Topical steroids (although effective but are **rarely needed**)\" | ⚠️ **The hard distractor — and it is not wrong, it is unnecessary.** The book concedes efficacy in the same breath as it withholds the indication |\n| **C. Topical anti-histamines** | \"Topical mast cell stabilizers and antihistamines\" | The key |\n| **D. Antibiotics** | Bacterial disease only | The condition is allergic; nothing here is infective |\n\n**B is the option to be able to argue against, because the book half-endorses it.** *Rarely needed* is the whole objection: a mild papillary reaction with injection and no corneal sign does not warrant a steroid, and the patient is two years old, so any avoidable steroid exposure is a poor trade. Compare the bank's own `opmcq-c6-1`, which asks the *preventive* drug in the same disease and keys the mast cell stabiliser — **the same four-line list answers both questions, and which line you take depends on the verb in the stem.**\n\n**Note what the antihistamine is doing here and not doing in `opmcq-c6-1`.** This stem asks for management of a patient who is symptomatic now, and an antihistamine acts on mediators already released. `opmcq-c6-1` asks how to *prevent* future episodes, and the stabiliser acts before release. Both drugs sit on the same printed line of p.87; the stem's tense chooses between them.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-266` (\"**Vernal conjunctivitis** is **not** treated with:\") is the nearest Grade Gain treatment question, but it is vernal rather than seasonal disease; no Grade Gain topic-6 question asks the management of seasonal allergic conjunctivitis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Seasonal allergic conjunctivitis is managed with cold compresses, artificial tears and topical mast cell stabilisers or antihistamines; the book calls topical steroids effective but rarely needed, so they are the wrong answer rather than a wrong drug.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.45 (Ch.6 Q13; the stem is printed "In the previous case, what is the most appropriate line of management?" and refers back to Ch.6 Q12. The key line prints directly beneath option D with no blank line — the only question in the chapter laid out that way)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"All of the following are among the characteristics of cicatricial trachoma except?",
    options:["Linear or stellate conjunctival scars","Herbert pits","Post trachomatous concretions","Superficial corneal vascularization and cellular infiltration"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints the keyed option as \"Superficial conreal vascularization\"** — *conreal* for *corneal*. Repaired above; the misprint is recorded in the staging file. ⚠️ **The identical typo recurs in `opmcq-c6-17`'s stem**, so it is a habit of this chapter's typesetting, not a one-off.\n\n⚠️ **This question's key is the chapter's only orphaned answer line.** Its stem and all four options print at the foot of book p.45; the bare line \"Answer: D\" opens book p.46. Only the key crossed the break — no option text moved, and the question's shape is intact.\n\n**Options A, B and C are the cicatricial row of the p.92–93 table; option D is the parenthesis that defines PANNUS in the active row.** `ophthalmology.pdf`:\n\n> **Cicatricial trachoma** — \"prevalent in **middle age**\":\n> **Conjunctival scarring:** – In mild cases → **Linear or stellate conjunctival scars** – In severe disease → Broad confluent scars (**Arlt's line**)\n> **Herbert pits:** Superior limbal follicles may resolve to leave a row of shallow depressions\n> (p.93) – Trichiasis, corneal vascularization and **cicatricial entropion** – Severe corneal opacification · **Post-trachomatous concretions (PTCs):** cause chronic irritation and foreign body sensation\n>\n> **Active trachoma:** ... ▪ **pannus** formation (**superficial corneal vascularization and cellular infiltration**)\n\nOption D is that parenthesis, word for word. So **D**.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **THIS IS THE EXACT MIRROR OF `opmcq-c6-9`**, five questions earlier, which asks the *active* half and keys **Herbert pits** — option B here. The same table read in both directions, with each question's key drawn from the other's stage. Get the table's two rows straight and both questions fall; confuse them and both fail together.\n\n| Option | Stage | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Linear or stellate conjunctival scars** | Cicatricial | The mild end of conjunctival scarring; Arlt's line is the severe end |\n| **B. Herbert pits** | Cicatricial | Limbal follicles that have **resolved** into shallow depressions — a healed lesion |\n| **C. Post trachomatous concretions** | Cicatricial | The PTCs of p.93, a late sequela causing chronic irritation |\n| **D. Superficial corneal vascularization and cellular infiltration** | **ACTIVE** | This is **pannus**, and the book gives it to the active row — the key |\n\n**The one word that carries D is *cellular infiltration*.** Vascularisation alone would be ambiguous: p.93 lists \"corneal vascularization\" among the cicatricial complications too. What makes D active is the **infiltrate** — living inflammatory cells in the cornea, which is inflammation happening now rather than scar left behind. That distinction is the whole question, and the book never states it in words; it is carried entirely by which row the phrase sits in.\n\n**The full cicatricial list, worth reproducing in a written paper:** linear or stellate scars → broad confluent scars (**Arlt's line**) → **Herbert pits** → trichiasis → **cicatricial entropion** → corneal vascularisation → severe corneal opacification → **PTCs**. Scarring \"is most prominent on the **upper tarsal plate**\".\n\n⚠️ **None of these eponyms is taught in the lectures.** Measured case-insensitively across all 29 cached decks: every `Arlt` hit is either an absence register (`L5` line 1295, `L6` line 1126) or a different structure entirely — `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination.txt` line 411 uses \"Arlt's line\" as a name for the **grey line of the lid margin**, which is not the trachomatous scar at all. ⚠️ **One name, two meanings, both inside this module's own sources.** `L6`'s nine trachoma slides (25–33) photograph pannus, nebula ex pannus, PTCs and scarring, and name none of the signs.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-261` (\"All of the following may be seen with **trachoma** except:\") and `opqb-t6-242` (\"**Complications of trachoma** include:\") work the same table; `opqb-t6-285` (\"Blood vessels in a **trachomatous pannus** lie:\") asks the anatomy of the very lesion this question keys. ⚠️ `opqb-t6-230` calls Herbert pits \"**Herbert's rosettes**\" — a third name for option B.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Cicatricial trachoma is scars, Arlt's line, Herbert pits, trichiasis, cicatricial entropion, corneal opacity and post-trachomatous concretions; pannus — superficial corneal vascularisation WITH cellular infiltration — belongs to the active stage.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.45 (Ch.6 Q14; option D is printed "Superficial conreal vascularization". The key "Answer: D" is orphaned onto book p.46 — the chapter\'s only orphaned answer line)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"Which of the following is a mandatory step to conduct in managing a patient with subconjunctival hemorrhage?",
    options:["CT orbit","Intraocular pressure measuring","Systemic blood pressure measuring","All of the above"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Two independent sources in this module print the same instruction, and neither prints the other three.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.95, management of subconjunctival haemorrhage:\n\n> - Assurance of the patient if there are no other ocular findings\n> - **Blood pressure measurement** and systemic workup to detect the cause.\n> - It usually resolves spontaneously in **2-3 weeks**\n> - A large area of subconjunctival haemorrhage with a history of trauma should arouse high suspicion for **eye globe rupture**.\n\nand `L37) Red Eye.txt` line 639, independently:\n\n> \"**Blood pressure should be measured**, as marked elevation can result in subconjunctival hemorrhage.\"\n\nSo **C**.\n\n**Why blood pressure and not the eye.** The lesion itself is harmless — the book calls it \"Presence of blood under the conjunctiva, otherwise asymptomatic\", with no pain, no visual loss and no discharge. The value of the encounter is not in treating the eye but in **finding the systemic cause**, and the book's own cause list is systemic more often than ocular: idiopathic, trauma, Valsalva manoeuvre, **bleeding disorders**, **hypertension**, **anticoagulation therapy**. Hypertension is the one that is common, silent, and dangerous if missed — so measuring it is the step that changes outcomes.\n\n| Option | When the course would do it | Why not mandatory |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. CT orbit** | Not mentioned anywhere in chapter 6 | No imaging is printed for this condition. Even the globe-rupture concern is handled by **referral**, not by a scan the reader orders |\n| **B. Intraocular pressure measuring** | Not printed for this condition | ⚠️ Tempting because a **low** IOP after trauma suggests rupture — which is exactly what `opmcq-c6-27` keys — but that is the traumatic subset, not every patient, and the book never lists IOP as a step here |\n| **C. Systemic blood pressure measuring** | p.95, and `L37` line 639 | The key |\n| **D. All of the above** | — | Fails on A |\n\n**B is the distractor with a real argument behind it**, and this bank makes the argument itself two pages later. `opmcq-c6-27` gives a trauma patient with a large subconjunctival haemorrhage and a **digitally measured IOP lower than normal**, and keys globe rupture. So IOP does matter — *in trauma*. This stem gives no trauma and no qualifier, and the word is **mandatory**: the step required of every patient. Only the blood pressure qualifies.\n\n⚠️ **The tutorial box on the same page adds two more steps, and neither is offered.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.95 prints a dashed Tutorial box: artificial tears for discomfort, **check the INR if the patient is on an anticoagulant**, and **if recurrent, investigate for a bleeding disorder**. `L37` line 637 agrees on the recurrence rule — \"If recurrent, an underlying bleeding disorder should be considered.\" Worth carrying, but tutorial-marked in the book, which the contents page says to study in round and skip in the final.\n\n⚠️ **The two sources disagree on the natural history and both are recorded.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.95 says it \"usually resolves spontaneously in **2-3 weeks**\"; `L37` line 772 says \"the hemorrhage will resolve in **1 to 2 weeks** without any damage to the eye\". Neither is corrected and neither is asked here.\n\n⚠️ **The lecture gives it one slide.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` slide 65 is titled \"Subconjunctival hemorrhage\" and carries four photographs and no text.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-248` (\"A **subconjunctival hemorrhage**:\") is the closest Grade Gain question and is a general-truth stem rather than a management stem; `opqb-t6-283` (severe head trauma, a large triangular subconjunctival haemorrhage coming from the fornix, next investigation) is the traumatic counterpart and pairs with `opmcq-c6-27` rather than with this.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Subconjunctival haemorrhage is harmless in itself; the mandatory step is measuring the systemic blood pressure, because hypertension, bleeding disorders and anticoagulation are the causes worth finding.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.46 (Ch.6 Q15)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"All of the following can cause conjunctival papillae except?",
    options:["Allergic conjunctivitis","Foreign bodies","Chlamydia","Contact lenses"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The intended reasoning is the follicle-versus-papilla split, and three of the four options come off one book list.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.86:\n\n> **8. Conjunctival papillae:** They have a **fibrovascular core** (in contrast to follicles) surrounded by hyperplastic conjunctival epithelium. They develop with *bacterial conjunctivitis* and *chronic blepharitis*. Giant papillae (**Cobble stone appearance**) are those **more than 1mm in diameter**... as in:\n> 1. **Allergic conjunctivitis** especially in vernal catarrh\n> 2. Prolonged **contact lens** wear\n> 3. **Foreign body** (as in artificial eye users)\n\nOptions A, D and B are items 1, 2 and 3 in that order. **Chlamydia is not on the list, and chlamydial disease is the classic cause of the OTHER lesion** — p.86 item 7 gives follicles to \"Viral conjunctivitis · **Chlamydia conjunctivitis (trachoma)** · Hypersensitivity to topical medications (e.g., Brimonidine)\". So the key is **C**.\n\n| Lesion | Core | Causes, as the book prints them |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Follicle** | Lymphoid — \"leucocytic infiltration (mainly **lymphocytes**)\", vessels running **around** it | Viral · **Chlamydial / trachoma** · drug hypersensitivity (brimonidine) · folliculosis in children *(tutorial)* |\n| **Papilla** | **Fibrovascular** core, hyperplastic epithelium | Bacterial · chronic blepharitis · **allergic (esp. vernal)** · **contact lens** · **foreign body** |\n\n⚠️⚠️ **DIVERGENCE — RECORDED, NOT DISPUTED. The key is right about the teaching and wrong about the book's own text, and this bank contradicts itself on the point.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.91 prints, under adult chlamydial conjunctivitis:\n\n> Signs: - Mucopurulent discharge - Tender preauricular lymph nodes - Conjunctival **follicles** (upper and lower tarsal) - **Conjunctival papillae in chronic cases**\n\nand p.92 prints, under **active trachoma** — which is chlamydial disease:\n\n> o **Papillary reaction** can also be present\n\n**So chlamydia can cause papillae, twice on the book's own pages** — and this same bank keys \"Papillary reaction\" as a *characteristic of active trachoma* in `opmcq-c6-9`, option D. Read strictly, option C is true and the question has no exception.\n\n**The key stands and is not disputed.** `answer` is the bank's printed key, always. What the question is testing is the *characteristic* association — the lesion each disease is known by — and by that reading chlamydia belongs to follicles and the other three to papillae. The strict-reading conflict is recorded here so a student who spots it knows they were right to spot it. ⚠️ **In a written paper, name the follicle as chlamydia's characteristic sign and add that papillae appear in chronic cases; that answer is defensible against both readings.**\n\n**The 1 mm threshold is the number to carry**, because it separates ordinary papillae from the giant cobblestone papillae of vernal disease that `opmcq-c6-10` and `opmcq-c6-12` turn on. The book teaches the size difference mainly in two photographs (p.86, \"Giant papillary conjunctivitis\" and \"papillary conjunctivitis\"); its cache flags this as figure-only teaching, since the prose gives only the number.\n\n⚠️ **Not in the lecture.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` gives signs of conjunctival disease slides 8–16, including discharge, injection, chemosis, follicles, papillae, PTCs and scarring — all photographs under titles, with no definition, no core, no cause list and no threshold.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-268` (\"**Conjunctival follicles** are present in the following **except**:\") is the follicle mirror of this question and the closest match; `opqb-t6-272` (\"**Cobble stone papillae** are pathognomonic of:\") and `opqb-t6-241` (\"**Papillae** of spring catarrh have the following characteristics **EXCEPT**:\") work the same p.86 block.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Papillae have a fibrovascular core and follow allergic disease, contact lenses, foreign bodies, bacterial conjunctivitis and chronic blepharitis; follicles are lymphoid and characteristic of viral, chlamydial and drug-induced disease — though the book does print papillae in chronic chlamydial cases.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.46 (Ch.6 Q16)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"Damage to which of the following structures causes corneal superficial vascularization and conjunctivalization of corneal epithelium?",
    options:["Limbus","Sclera","Cornea","Conjunctiva"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints \"conjunctivalization of conreal epithelium\"** — *conreal* for *corneal*. Repaired above; the misprint is recorded in the staging file, and it is the second appearance of the identical typo after `opmcq-c6-14`'s option D.\n\n**The stem is a near-verbatim lift of the last anatomy bullet in the chapter.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.83:\n\n> • The conjunctiva at the **limbus** hosts the **palisades of Vogt** which contain the **stem cells** that provide regenerative cells to the corneal epithelium. **Damage to these structures results in corneal stem cell deficiency with corneal superficial vascularization and conjunctivalization of the corneal epithelium.**\n\n\"These structures\" are the palisades of Vogt, and they sit **at the limbus**. So **A**.\n\n**The mechanism in one line:** the corneal epithelium does not renew itself from within. Its stem cells live in a ring at the limbus, and that ring also acts as a barrier. Destroy it and two things follow together — nothing replaces the corneal epithelium, so **conjunctival** epithelium creeps across instead (*conjunctivalisation*), and it brings **blood vessels** with it, because conjunctiva is vascular and cornea is not. Both halves of the stem are one lesion.\n\n| Option | What it is | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Limbus** | The corneoscleral junction, where the palisades of Vogt and the stem cells sit | The key |\n| **B. Sclera** | Opaque outer coat; the bulbar conjunctiva lies loosely on it | Holds no stem cells and no role in corneal renewal |\n| **C. Cornea** | The tissue that *suffers* the consequence | ⚠️ **The subtle trap: the cornea is the victim, not the site of the damage.** Damaging the cornea itself gives an ulcer or a scar, not stem cell deficiency |\n| **D. Conjunctiva** | The tissue that *invades* | Conjunctivalisation is conjunctiva doing what it always does when the barrier fails — it is the result, not the cause |\n\n**C and D are both consequences dressed as causes**, which is the whole design of the menu: three of the four options name tissues that appear in the stem's own description of the outcome. Only the limbus is upstream of it.\n\n**Where this reappears in the course.** The same lesion is printed under VKC on p.88 — \"Limbal disease can result in a **limbal stem cell deficiency** which can lead to *corneal neovascularization*\" — so chronic vernal disease is one route to the state this question describes, and Tranta spots sit on that same limbus.\n\n⚠️ **Book-only, measured.** `palisade` returns **zero hits across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks**. `Vogt` returns two live hits and neither is this: `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera.txt` line 665 records **VOGT–KOYANAGI–HARADA syndrome as \"Entirely absent\"**, and `L15,16) Cornea.txt` line 266 has **Vogt's striae**, the vertical stromal stress lines of keratoconus — a different Vogt structure in a different tissue. ⚠️ **Two unrelated eponymous \"Vogt\" findings inside this module's own sources; do not conflate them.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt`'s anatomy slides 2–7 are labelled diagrams, one of them German-labelled, and none names the palisades.\n\n**Cross-bank:** no Grade Gain topic-6 question asks limbal stem cells or the palisades of Vogt — checked across all 63 stems. `opqb-t6-244` (\"A **clear zone between the lesion and the limbus** is **NOT** present in:\") is the only limbus question and it is about lesion position, not stem cells.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The corneal epithelial stem cells live in the palisades of Vogt at the limbus; destroying them gives stem cell deficiency, so conjunctival epithelium and its vessels advance across the cornea.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.46 (Ch.6 Q17; the stem is printed "conjunctivalization of conreal epithelium")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"Preauricular lymphadenopathy occurs in all of the following cases except?",
    options:["Viral conjunctivitis","Streptococcal conjunctivitis","Chlamydial conjunctivitis","Severe gonococcal conjunctivitis"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The book prints a three-bullet list and the bank has asked which option is not on it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.86, clinical feature 9:\n\n> **9. Preauricular lymphadenopathy (± submandibular lymphadenopathy):**\n> ▪ **Viral** conjunctivitis\n> ▪ **Chlamydial** conjunctivitis\n> ▪ **Severe bacterial** conjunctivitis (especially **gonococcal**)\n\nOptions A, C and D are those three bullets in the book's own order, with the third rendered as its parenthetical example. **B, streptococcal, is the inserted distractor.** So **B**.\n\n| Option | On the p.86 list? | Corroboration elsewhere in the chapter |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Viral** | Yes | p.90 repeats \"Preauricular lymphadenopathy\" among viral signs; p.94's DD grid gives viral \"Follicles + preauricular lymphadenopathy\" |\n| **B. Streptococcal** | **No** | ⚠️ *Streptococcus* is not named anywhere in chapter 6 — see below. The key |\n| **C. Chlamydial** | Yes | p.91 gives adult chlamydial disease \"**Tender** preauricular lymph nodes\" |\n| **D. Severe gonococcal** | Yes | p.91: \"Pre-auricular and submandibular lymphadenopathy in **gonococcal and meningococcal** infections\" |\n\n**The rule the list encodes is worth stating in one sentence:** ordinary bacterial conjunctivitis does **not** give a preauricular node, and that absence is one of the things separating it from viral disease on the p.94 grid. A node in a bacterial case means the infection is severe — gonococcal or meningococcal. Streptococcal conjunctivitis, if it occurs, has no reason to behave differently from any other ordinary bacterial conjunctivitis.\n\n⚠️ **Option B names an organism the chapter never names, and that is measurable.** The book's four common causes of acute bacterial conjunctivitis are \"**H. influenzae, S. pneumoniae, S. aureus and Moraxella catarrhalis**\" (p.91) — *S. pneumoniae* is a **Streptococcus**, so \"streptococcal conjunctivitis\" is not a fictitious entity; it is one of the commonest forms of the ordinary disease. What makes B the answer is not that streptococci cannot infect the conjunctiva but that **ordinary bacterial conjunctivitis carries no preauricular node**. Reading B as an invented distractor gets the right answer for the wrong reason.\n\n⚠️ **Note the asymmetry the bank exploits.** Option D is qualified — *severe* gonococcal — and option B is not. The book's own bullet is qualified the same way: \"**Severe** bacterial conjunctivitis (especially gonococcal)\". An unqualified bacterial option is off the list; a severity-qualified one is on it.\n\n⚠️ **Not in the lecture.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` names **no organism in 66 slides** — measured, and recorded in the deck's own register at line 1115: `gonorrhea = 0 · Haemophil = 0 · Koch-Weeks = 0 · Morax / Moraxella = 0`. Its bacterial section, slides 17–24, is eight photographs under titles.\n\n**Cross-bank:** no Grade Gain topic-6 question asks the preauricular lymphadenopathy list — checked across all 63 stems. `opqb-t6-243` (\"**Staphylococcal** infections in ophthalmology do **NOT** include:\") is the nearest organism-exclusion question and is about a different organism and a different list.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A preauricular node belongs to viral and chlamydial conjunctivitis and to severe bacterial disease, especially gonococcal; ordinary bacterial conjunctivitis, streptococcal included, does not produce one.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.46 (Ch.6 Q18)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most common external ocular infection?",
    options:["Viral conjunctivitis","Bacterial conjunctivitis","Fungal conjunctivitis","Parasitic conjunctivitis"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The stem quotes the book's own words back at you.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.90 opens the viral-conjunctivitis section with:\n\n> • **The most common external ocular infection**\n> • **Adenovirus** is the most frequent causative agent (90% of cases)\n\nThat first bullet is the answer, verbatim. So **A**.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **THIS QUESTION AND `opmcq-c6-25` LOOK CONTRADICTORY AND ARE NOT.** Six questions later the bank asks \"Which of the following is the most common cause of conjunctivitis?\" and keys **allergic**. Both keys are correct, because the two stems ask different questions, and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.84 supports both in one aetiology block:\n\n| Stem asks | Key | The book's line |\n|---|---|---|\n| Most common **external ocular INFECTION** (this question) | **Viral** | p.90: \"The most common external ocular infection\" |\n| Most common **cause of CONJUNCTIVITIS** (`opmcq-c6-25`) | **Allergic** | p.84: allergic is \"The **most frequent cause** of conjunctivitis, affecting **15-40%** of the population\" |\n\n**The bridging sentence is on p.84 and it is the one to memorise:** \"Viruses and bacteria are the most common **infectious** causes with **viruses being the leading cause of infectious conjunctivitis**.\" Allergic disease is not an infection, so it wins the wider question and does not compete for this one. **Read the noun in the stem before answering either.**\n\n| Option | The book's treatment of it |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Viral** | p.90, the whole section — 90 % adenoviral, highly contagious, epidemic. The key |\n| **B. Bacterial** | p.91, a full section, but second to viral among infectious causes |\n| **C. Fungal** | ⚠️ Named **once** in the whole chapter, in the aetiology list on p.84 (\"Bacterial, viral, chlamydial, fungal, parasitic\"), and never developed |\n| **D. Parasitic** | ⚠️ Same — named once in that same list and never developed |\n\n**C and D are filler and the book itself tells you so.** Chapter 6's not-covered register records them as \"named once in the aetiology list on printed 84 and never developed\". A cause the book mentions once cannot be the commonest anything.\n\n⚠️ **The lecture cannot answer this either.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` gives viral conjunctivitis three slides (34–36), all photographs; `adenovirus` returns zero live hits in that file. Book answer.\n\n**Cross-bank:** no Grade Gain topic-6 question asks either frequency claim — checked across all 63 stems.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Viral conjunctivitis is the most common external ocular infection and adenovirus causes 90 % of it — but allergic disease is the most common cause of conjunctivitis overall, because it is not an infection.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.46 (Ch.6 Q19)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"All of the following are true regarding conjunctival follicles except?",
    options:["It's mainly caused by viruses and chlamydia","It's a leukocytic infiltration mainly composed of macrophages","It may be caused by drugs as brimonidine","None of the above"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**One word is wrong, and it is the cell.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.86, clinical feature 7:\n\n> **7. Conjunctival follicles:**\n> o Multiple discrete slightly elevated lesions **with blood vessels running around them**\n> o Consist of **leucocytic infiltration (mainly lymphocytes)**\n> o **Causes:** 1. Viral conjunctivitis 2. Chlamydia conjunctivitis (trachoma) 3. Hypersensitivity to topical medications (e.g., **Brimonidine**) 4. *Folliculosis in children* **(tutorial)**\n\nOption B keeps the book's phrase \"leukocytic infiltration\" and swaps **lymphocytes** for **macrophages**. So **B**.\n\n| Option | The book's line | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Mainly caused by viruses and chlamydia** | Causes 1 and 2 of four, the two non-tutorial infective ones | True |\n| **B. Leukocytic infiltration mainly composed of macrophages** | \"mainly **lymphocytes**\" | **FALSE — the key** |\n| **C. May be caused by drugs as brimonidine** | Cause 3, and the book names brimonidine again on p.84 under toxic aetiology | True |\n| **D. None of the above** | — | Fails, because B is false |\n\n**A follicle is lymphoid tissue, and that is the fact behind the fact.** It is a germinal centre — organised lymphocytes — which is why follicles belong to the diseases that provoke a lymphocytic response: viruses, chlamydia, drug hypersensitivity. A macrophage-rich lesion would be a granuloma, which is a different thing entirely. The contrast with the papilla is structural, and the book draws it in the very next feature: a papilla has a **fibrovascular core** with vessels running **through** it, a follicle has a lymphoid core with vessels running **around** it.\n\n| | Follicle | Papilla |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core | Lymphoid — **mainly lymphocytes** | **Fibrovascular** |\n| Vessels | **Around** the lesion | Through the centre |\n| Characteristic causes | Viral · chlamydial · drug (brimonidine) | Allergic · contact lens · foreign body · bacterial · chronic blepharitis |\n\n**The related fact on the same bullet is worth carrying**, because it explains a corneal sign: \"Lymphocytic infiltration may, also, take place **deep to the corneal epithelium** (corneal **subepithelial infiltrates** as in **epidemic keratoconjunctivitis** caused by Adenovirus infection)\". The subepithelial infiltrates of EKC are the same lymphocytes, in the cornea.\n\n⚠️ **Brimonidine is the one drug in this question, and a second source names it live.** `L37) Red Eye.txt` line 592: \"the glaucoma medication **brimonidine** can produce a red eye with erythematous, swollen eyelids that have a dry 'leathery' texture\" — that is contact dermatitis rather than follicular conjunctivitis, so the two sources give the same drug two different ocular reactions. Both recorded.\n\n⚠️ **The tutorial fourth cause.** \"Folliculosis in children\" carries the book's tutorial marker, so it is study-in-round and skip-in-final material. It is the reason a healthy child can have follicles with no disease at all.\n\n⚠️ **Not in the lecture.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` slides 8–16 photograph follicles and papillae and define neither.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-268`, \"**Conjunctival follicles** are present in the following **except**:\", is the direct cause-list twin; `opqb-t6-275` (\"A boy 11 years old developed **acute follicular** conjunctivitis. The causative organism may be one of the following **except**:\") asks the same list through an organism menu.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A follicle is a lymphoid lesion — leucocytic infiltration mainly of LYMPHOCYTES with vessels running around it — caused by viruses, chlamydia and drugs such as brimonidine; macrophages belong to a granuloma, not a follicle.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.47 (Ch.6 Q20)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-21', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"What is the medication that is used to treat resistant cases of vernal keratoconjunctivitis?",
    options:["Local decongestants","Anti-histamines","Mast cell stabilizers","Topical tacrolimus"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**All four options are real VKC drugs, so this is a severity question, not a drug-recall question.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.89 prints the treatment as a five-line topical list, and the last line is the reserve:\n\n> ▪ mast cell stabilizers\n> ▪ antihistamines.\n> ▪ **vasoconstrictor** and anti-histaminic drops (**during the attack only**)\n> ▪ steroid to control acute attacks but **avoid long-term use**\n> ▪ **cyclosporine or topical tacrolimus may be considered for SEVERE CASES and as steroid-sparing agents**\n\nOnly the last line carries a severity qualifier, and it names tacrolimus. So **D**.\n\n| Option | Where it sits on the book's list | Why not \"resistant\" |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Local decongestants** | The vasoconstrictor line, \"during the attack **only**\" | Symptomatic and short-term — the opposite of a drug for resistant disease |\n| **B. Anti-histamines** | Line two, and line three in combination | First-line. `opmcq-c6-11` keys exactly this for the ordinary case |\n| **C. Mast cell stabilizers** | Line one | First-line and preventive; `opmcq-c6-1` keys it for seasonal prophylaxis |\n| **D. Topical tacrolimus** | Line five, \"for **severe cases** and as **steroid-sparing** agents\" | The key |\n\n**Two ideas are packed into that fifth line and both are examinable.** *Severe cases* is the escalation; *steroid-sparing* is the reason the escalation exists. The book has already restricted steroids to \"control acute attacks but avoid long-term use\" — so in a disease that recurs every spring for a decade, a drug that controls inflammation without a steroid's long-term cost is what resistant disease needs. **A calcineurin inhibitor suppresses T-cell activation locally, which is why it substitutes for a steroid rather than adding to one** — *that mechanism is **not taken from the course material**; the book names the drug and its role and explains neither.*\n\n⚠️ **Cyclosporine shares the line and is not an option here.** The book prints \"cyclosporine **or** topical tacrolimus\", so either would answer the stem. Only tacrolimus is on the menu. ⚠️ Note that `opmcq-c6-11` used **systemic** cyclosporine as a *distractor* — the same drug, wrong route. This chapter tests the pair twice and in opposite directions.\n\n⚠️ **Tacrolimus is taught nowhere in the lectures, measured.** `tacrolimus` returns **zero live hits across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks**; its single appearance is inside `L6) Conjunctiva.txt`'s own not-covered register at line 1149, on a line reading `olopatadine = 0 · antihistamin = 0 · cyclosporin = 0 · tacrolimus = 0`. `L6`'s nine allergic slides (37–45) are photographs. ⚠️ And the only anti-allergy drugs the **book** names anywhere in the chapter are **ketotifen and olopatadine**, and it names them under **AKC**, not under VKC.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-266` (\"**Vernal conjunctivitis** is **not** treated with:\") is the closest Grade Gain question and asks the same list as an exclusion; `opqb-t6-225` (a boy on long-term spring-catarrh treatment who develops defective vision) tests the steroid complication that makes a steroid-sparing agent necessary.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Every VKC drug is topical; mast cell stabilisers and antihistamines are first-line, decongestants are for the attack only, steroids are short-course, and cyclosporine or topical tacrolimus is the steroid-sparing reserve for severe or resistant disease.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.47 (Ch.6 Q21)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-22', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 25-year-old male patient suffering from bronchial asthma and conjunctivitis. Which of the following is commonly associated with his condition?",
    options:["Atopy","Family history","Viral infection","All of the above"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The stem is a sentence fragment as printed** — \"A 25-year-old male patient suffering from bronchial asthma and conjunctivitis.\" with no main verb. Transcribed verbatim above; the meaning is not in doubt and repairing the grammar would change the bank's text without changing the question.\n\n**Asthma plus conjunctivitis is the atopic axis, and the book prints the association twice.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.87, inside the tutorial-marked introduction to allergic conjunctivitis:\n\n> Allergic conjunctivitis is usually **associated with allergic rhinitis, eczema or asthma.**\n\nand p.89, under atopic keratoconjunctivitis:\n\n> - Usually there is history of **atopic dermatitis (eczema) or bronchial asthma.**\n\n**Atopy is the name of that triad** — asthma, eczema, allergic rhinitis — and conjunctivitis is its ocular member. So **A**.\n\n| Option | The book's position | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Atopy** | The p.87 and p.89 associations, and the disease name itself: **Atopic** keratoconjunctivitis | The key |\n| **B. Family history** | ⚠️ **Not printed anywhere in chapter 6.** No inheritance, no family clustering, no genetics | Defensible in general allergy; **absent from this course**, which is what makes D fail |\n| **C. Viral infection** | Viral disease is a separate section (p.90) with no allergic link | Nothing connects asthma to a viral conjunctivitis |\n| **D. All of the above** | — | Fails on B and C together |\n\n**D is the reflex answer and B is the reason it fails.** Atopic disease does run in families, and a student answering from general medical knowledge will take D. The course does not print it: measured, chapter 6 contains no family history, no inheritance and — its own not-covered register records this — **no immunology at all**, \"no hypersensitivity type (I / IV)\", \"no IgE, no mast-cell mediator list beyond the phrase 'mast cell degranulation'\". *(That atopy is a heritable IgE-mediated tendency is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n⚠️ **Which allergic disease is this patient likely to have?** The stem gives an adult with asthma, which points at **atopic keratoconjunctivitis** — p.89 makes AKC \"a rare bilateral condition that affects **adults (30-50 years)**\", \"Usually there is history of atopic dermatitis (eczema) or bronchial asthma\", perennial and **worse in the winter**. At 25 he is below that range, which is why the bank asks for the *association* rather than the diagnosis. **The question is answerable without naming the disease, and that is deliberate.**\n\n**The AKC signs worth carrying, since this stem gestures at them:** eyelid eczema with redness, scaling and dryness, **associated with staphylococcal blepharitis**; conjunctiva more watery than in VKC with **small** papillae; cornea with superficial punctate erosions, persistent epithelial defects, and **keratoconus is common**. Its symptoms are \"Similar to VKC but *more severe*\".\n\n⚠️ **Not in the lecture.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` names no systemic association anywhere in 66 slides; its allergic section is nine photographic slides (37–45).\n\n**Cross-bank:** no Grade Gain topic-6 question asks the atopic association — checked across all 63 stems. `opqb-t6-259` (a 50-year-old with **rheumatoid arthritis**, red eye, tearing and burning) is the other systemic-association vignette in the topic and is a dry-eye/scleral question, not an allergic one.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Allergic conjunctivitis is associated with allergic rhinitis, eczema and asthma — the atopic triad — and the book prints no family history and no immunology, so atopy alone is the answer.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.47 (Ch.6 Q22; the stem is printed as a sentence fragment)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-23', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"According to the WHO trachoma grading, which of the following is meant by TS?",
    options:["5 or more follicles on the superior tarsal plate","Diffuse involvement of tarsal conjunctiva","Easily visible fibrous white tarsal bands","At least one lash touching the globe","Corneal opacification"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"⚠️⚠️ **THE BANK MISPRINTS THE KEYED OPTION.** It prints \"**Early** visible fibrous white tarsal bands\"; `ophthalmology.pdf` p.93 prints \"TS = Trachomatous conjunctival scarring: **Easily** visible fibrous white tarsal bands\". *Early* for *Easily*. Repaired above; the misprinted word is recorded here and in the staging file, and **the key does not move** — option C is the answer either way. ⚠️ The same misprint sits, unkeyed, in `opmcq-c6-8`'s option C.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **THIS QUESTION AND `opmcq-c6-8` PRINT AN IDENTICAL FIVE-OPTION MENU** — same wording, same order, same misprint — and differ only in the two-letter grade the stem names: **TS** here, **TT** there. Verified character by character at 2.2× zoom on book pp.44 and 47. They sit fifteen questions apart, so the pairing is invisible in a linear read and the failure mode is answering from memory of the other stem.\n\n**The five WHO grades, `ophthalmology.pdf` p.93:**\n\n| Grade | Stands for | Definition | Option |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **TF** | Trachomatous inflammation (**follicular**) | 5 or more follicles **> 0.5 mm** on the superior tarsal plate | A |\n| **TI** | Trachomatous inflammation (**intense**) | Diffuse involvement of the tarsal conjunctiva obscuring **50 % or more** of the deep tarsal vessels; papillae present | B |\n| **TS** | Trachomatous conjunctival **Scarring** | **Easily visible fibrous white tarsal bands** | **C — the key** |\n| **TT** | Trachomatous **Trichiasis** | At least one lash touching the globe | D |\n| **CO** | **Corneal Opacity** | Sufficient to blur details of at least part of the pupillary margin | E |\n\n**The mnemonic is the second letter every time:** T**F** follicular, T**I** intense, T**S** scarring, T**T** trichiasis, **CO** corneal opacity. TS is where the disease crosses from active to cicatricial — the follicles are gone and the white bands they left are visible on the everted upper tarsus. **Broad confluent scars at that site are Arlt's line** (p.92), which is TS in its severe form.\n\n**Why the misprint matters even though the key survives it.** *Easily visible* is a **detectability threshold** — it exists so that a field worker with a loupe can grade a village consistently. *Early visible* would make it a **timing** statement, which is exactly wrong: TS is a late finding, prevalent in middle age. A student who memorises the bank's wording learns the grade with its logic inverted.\n\n**The grading system exists to drive the SAFE strategy on the same page:** **S**urgery for trichiasis and complications, **A**ntibiotics for active disease, **F**acial hygiene, **E**nvironmental improvement. TT triggers the S; TF and TI trigger the A.\n\n⚠️ **Book-only, measured.** `\"SAFE strategy\"`, `\"face washing\"` and `MacCallan` all return **zero live hits** in `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` — the hits are its own not-covered register at lines 1104 and 1170. The deck's nine trachoma slides (25–33) photograph follicles, pannus, nebula ex pannus, PTCs and scarring, and print no grade letter.\n\n**Cross-bank: Grade Gain topic 6 asks the WHO grading nowhere** — checked across all 63 stems, `opqb-t6-224` to `opqb-t6-288`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"TS is trachomatous conjunctival scarring — easily visible fibrous white tarsal bands, the first cicatricial grade; TF and TI are the active grades and TT is trichiasis.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.47 (Ch.6 Q23; option C is printed "Early visible fibrous white tarsal bands", where ophthalmology.pdf p.93 prints "Easily visible fibrous white tarsal bands")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-24', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-red',
    stem:"Which of the following can cause eye redness without affecting vision?",
    options:["Conjunctivitis","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Central retinal artery occlusion","Iritis"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **Filed `op-red` rather than `op-conj`.** The key is conjunctivitis and the question sits in the conjunctiva chapter, but three of the four options are not conjunctival and the skill being tested is the red-eye triage. `op-conj` is the secondary chapter.\n\n**The book states the discriminator as a heading, and it is the only place it appears.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.94 heads its differential table:\n\n> **DD OF CONJUNCTIVITIS**\n> **Normal VA, Normal pupil, No photophobia, Clear cornea, Normal AC**\n\nFive negatives, and the first of them answers this question: **conjunctivitis leaves visual acuity normal.** So **A**.\n\n| Option | Red eye? | Vision | Why it fails the stem |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A. Conjunctivitis** | Yes | **Normal** | The key — the p.94 heading, in one word |\n| **B. Acute angle-closure glaucoma** | Yes | **Severely reduced**, with haloes | Pressure clouds the cornea; the pupil is mid-dilated and fixed and the eye is painful. It fails four of the five p.94 negatives at once |\n| **C. Central retinal artery occlusion** | ⚠️ **NO** | **Profoundly lost, painlessly** | ⚠️ **The odd one out: CRAO does not cause a red eye at all.** It is sudden painless monocular blindness with a white quiet eye. It fails the stem's *first* clause, not its second |\n| **D. Iritis** | Yes | Reduced, with **photophobia** | Anterior uveitis gives circumcorneal (ciliary) injection, a small irregular pupil, photophobia and cells in the anterior chamber — it fails four negatives |\n\n**The negatives are worth memorising as a set, because they are how a red eye is triaged.** *Normal VA · normal pupil · no photophobia · clear cornea · normal anterior chamber* — a red eye with **all five** is conjunctivitis and can be treated; a red eye missing **any one** of them is something else and needs an ophthalmologist. That is the whole of the book's approach in eleven words.\n\n**C is included for a different reason from B and D**, and noticing that is the point of the question. B and D are genuine red eyes that fail on vision. C is not a red eye at all, so a student reading only the second half of the stem may pass over it. **Both halves of the stem are doing work.**\n\n⚠️ **Redness and injection are not one thing, and the module's own sources say so.** `L37) Red Eye.txt` line 646 describes the conjunctivitis pattern as \"a diffuse dilation of the conjunctival blood vessels... **less intense in the perilimbal region**, in contrast to the **perilimbal dilation of deeper vessels characteristic of ciliary flush**\", and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.85 prints the same rule as \"Red eye (Conjunctival injection ***sparing the limbal area***)\". **Ciliary flush is the sign that pushes the diagnosis away from conjunctivitis and toward iritis or glaucoma.** The p.82 chapter-opener figure teaches exactly that pattern without captioning it.\n\n⚠️ **The material for this question is mostly `op-red`'s, not `op-conj`'s.** `L37) Red Eye.txt` is a book chapter of about 5,100 words and is the module's densest source on this differential; it also carries the referral thresholds. Chapter 6 supplies only the p.94 heading. Neither source is the lecture deck: `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` prints no differential at all.\n\n**Cross-bank:** no Grade Gain topic-6 question asks this differential — checked across all 63 stems. `opqb-t6-232` (\"A **painful, tender, non-itchy localized** redness of the conjunctiva can be due to:\") and `opqb-t6-245` (\"Causes of **localized** conjunctival redness include:\") are the nearest, and both ask about the *distribution* of redness rather than about vision.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Conjunctivitis is the red eye with normal VA, normal pupil, no photophobia, a clear cornea and a normal anterior chamber; angle-closure glaucoma and iritis are red eyes that fail those tests, and CRAO is not a red eye at all.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.47 (Ch.6 Q24)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-25', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"Which of the following is the most common cause of conjunctivitis?",
    options:["Viral","Bacterial","Allergic","Traumatic"],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book puts the answer in the first line of its aetiology section, with a number.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.84:\n\n> **Non-infectious causes:**\n> a) **Allergic:** The **most frequent cause** of conjunctivitis, affecting **15-40%** of the population and is observed more frequently in the spring and summer.\n\nSo **C**.\n\n⚠️⚠️ **THIS AND `opmcq-c6-19` ARE A DESIGNED PAIR AND THEIR KEYS ONLY LOOK INCOMPATIBLE.** Six questions earlier the bank asked \"Which of the following is the most common **external ocular infection**?\" and keyed **viral**. Both are correct, and the same p.84 block reconciles them:\n\n> **Infectious causes:** Bacterial, viral, chlamydial, fungal, parasitic — \"(Viruses and bacteria are the most common **infectious** causes with **viruses being the leading cause of infectious conjunctivitis**.)\"\n\n| Question | Universe of comparison | Key |\n|---|---|---|\n| `opmcq-c6-19` | **Infections** of the ocular surface | **Viral** |\n| This question | **All causes** of conjunctivitis, infective and not | **Allergic** |\n\n**Allergic disease wins the wider question precisely because it is not an infection**, so it never entered the comparison `opmcq-c6-19` was making. ⚠️ **Read the noun in the stem.** Two adjacent questions in one chapter, two different correct answers, and the difference is one word.\n\n| Option | The book's line | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Viral** | Leading **infectious** cause; 90 % of it adenoviral (p.90) | Second overall, first among infections |\n| **B. Bacterial** | Named with viruses as \"the most common infectious causes\" (p.84) | Behind viral even within infections |\n| **C. Allergic** | \"The most frequent cause... **15-40%** of the population\" | The key |\n| **D. Traumatic** | ⚠️ **Not a category the book uses.** p.84's non-infectious list is allergic, **toxic (chemical)**, and secondary to other disorders | A plausible-sounding category that does not exist in this taxonomy |\n\n**The full aetiology, worth reproducing whole**, because two of its branches are asked nowhere else in this chapter:\n\n- **Non-infectious:** allergic (15–40 %) · **toxic/chemical** — topical medications such as **brimonidine**, smoke, irradiation, chemicals, fumes · **secondary to other disorders** — local (dacryocystitis, dacryoadenitis, cellulitis) and systemic (immune-mediated disease, neoplastic processes)\n- **Infectious:** bacterial, viral, chlamydial, fungal, parasitic\n\n⚠️ **15–40 % is a prevalence of allergic conjunctivitis in the population, not a share of conjunctivitis cases**, which is how the book prints it. It is the only prevalence figure in the chapter. ⚠️ The book also notes that prevalence \"varies according to the underlying cause, which may be influenced by the patient's **age**, as well as the **season** of the year\" — so \"most common\" is season-dependent, and allergic disease is \"observed more frequently in the spring and summer\".\n\n⚠️ **Not in the lecture.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` prints no aetiology, no classification and no epidemiology anywhere in its 66 slides.\n\n**Cross-bank:** no Grade Gain topic-6 question asks either frequency claim — checked across all 63 stems.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Allergic disease is the most frequent cause of conjunctivitis overall, affecting 15-40 % of the population; viral is the leading INFECTIOUS cause, and the two claims live in the same paragraph.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.48 (Ch.6 Q25)' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-26', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 36-year-old male patient presents with a complaint of red eyes, itching and excessive lacrimation especially during spring and summer seasons. Examination revealed punctate epithelial corneal erosions and a mild degree of keratoconus. Which of the following is an additional finding that would probably be found in that patient?",
    options:["Hypopyon","Tranta spots","Dendritic corneal ulcer","Kayser-Fleischer ring"],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"⚠️ **Two repairs, both recorded.** The bank prints \"**presnets**\" for *presents* — the same typo as `opmcq-c6-10` — and the question clause as \"Which of the following **an additional finding that would be probably found** in that patient?\", which has no verb. Repaired above to \"is an additional finding that would probably be found\"; both misprints are in the staging file.\n\n⚠️ **This stem IS `opmcq-c6-10`'s stem**, minus *giant tarsal papillae*, with the age changed 23 → 36 and the question turned from diagnosis to additional-finding. About thirty words are shared verbatim, the typo included. **One template used twice inside this chapter** — not a cross-bank reprint.\n\n**The diagnosis is vernal keratoconjunctivitis and the question is what else to look for.** Seasonal itching, punctate epithelial erosions and keratoconus are three entries from the VKC sign list. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.88 divides those signs three ways — conjunctival, **limbal**, corneal — and the stem has given corneal signs and no limbal one:\n\n> **Limbal signs:** o Limbal papillae with whitish dots (**Tranta spots**) that consist of **degenerated eosinophils** o Limbal disease can result in a limbal stem cell deficiency which can lead to *corneal neovascularization*\n\nSo **B**.\n\n⚠️ **\"Tranta spots\" is NOT a misprint and is not corrected.** It is the book's own spelling, printed twice on p.88 — in the limbal-signs row and as the caption **\"Fig 6-4: Tranta spots\"**. ⚠️ **The lesion is spelt three ways across this module's own sources:** the book and House print **Tranta spots**, Grade Gain prints **Trant's spots** (`opqb-t6-231`), and the usual textbook form is *Horner-Trantas dots* — which appears in this module **only** inside `L6) Conjunctiva.txt`'s not-covered register at line 1123, recording \"Trantas (Horner–Trantas dots) = 0 hits, yet the chalky white limbal dots are\" photographed. **Three names, one lesion, and the deck that photographs it names it none of them.**\n\n| Option | What it is | Where the course puts it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Hypopyon** | Layered pus in the anterior chamber | A sign of severe intraocular inflammation or an infected corneal ulcer. **VKC is a surface allergy** — no anterior chamber involvement, and the p.94 DD grid gives conjunctivitis a *Normal AC* |\n| **B. Tranta spots** | Whitish limbal dots of **degenerated eosinophils** | p.88, VKC limbal signs — the key |\n| **C. Dendritic corneal ulcer** | The branching epithelial ulcer of **herpes simplex** keratitis | A different disease. `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.6 names HSV once, as \"Herpes simplex virus follicular conjunctivitis (blepharoconjunctivitis)\" (p.90), and prints no dendrite |\n| **D. Kayser-Fleischer ring** | Corneal copper deposition in **Wilson's disease** | ⚠️ Not named anywhere in chapter 6. A systemic-disease sign with no allergic connection |\n\n**A is the option worth pausing on**, because the stem does mention the cornea. But punctate epithelial erosions are an *epithelial* finding in an allergic eye; nothing in VKC breaches into the anterior chamber. A hypopyon in a patient like this would mean a secondary infected ulcer, which is a complication and not an \"additional finding\".\n\n**The full VKC sign set, by compartment — this is the table the question is drawn from:**\n\n| Compartment | Signs (`ophthalmology.pdf` pp.88–89) |\n|---|---|\n| **Conjunctival** | Diffuse injection · tarsal **giant papillae** (cobblestone) |\n| **Limbal** | **Tranta spots** — degenerated eosinophils · limbal stem cell deficiency → corneal neovascularisation |\n| **Corneal** | **Punctate epithelial erosions** · **shield ulcer**, oval, in the **upper 1/3**, plaque of fibrin and mucus · **pseudo-gerontoxon** (peripheral lipid) · **keratoconus** |\n\n⚠️ **The deck photographs Tranta spots across four slides and never names them.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` slides 39–42 are \"the limbal form with its chalky white limbal dots\", one of them with an arrow pointing at them, all untitled beyond the section heading. This is the clearest case in the chapter of a sign taught by picture and named only by the book.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-231`, \"**Trant's spots** are noticed in cases of:\", is the strongest single-fact match in this chapter — the same lesion asked in the opposite direction, with a third spelling. `opqb-t6-236` (\"In **vernal catarrh**, the characteristic cells are:\") asks the eosinophil that Tranta spots are made of; `opqb-t6-271` (a student rubbing his eyes, gelatinous limbal finding) and `opqb-t6-228` (mucoid nodules with a smooth rounded surface on the limbus) are the limbal-VKC vignettes.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"VKC signs come in three compartments — giant papillae conjunctivally, Tranta spots of degenerated eosinophils at the limbus, and punctate erosions, shield ulcer, pseudo-gerontoxon and keratoconus in the cornea; a stem giving corneal signs is asking for the limbal one.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.48 (Ch.6 Q26; the stem is printed "presnets" and "Which of the following an additional finding that would be probably found in that patient?")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-27', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"A 44-year-old female patient suffered a severe eye trauma that led to large subconjunctival hemorrhage. Digitally-measured IOP was lower than normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
    options:["Globe rupture","Retrobulbar hemorrhage","Hyphema","Iridodialysis"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"⚠️ **The bank prints \"Digitally- measured\", with the space after the hyphen.** Closed up above; recorded in the staging file. ⚠️ Filed `op-conj` because the sign the question turns on is the subconjunctival haemorrhage of `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.6; `op-trauma` is the secondary chapter.\n\n**The book prints this exact warning, as the last line of its subconjunctival-haemorrhage management.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.95:\n\n> - **A large area of subconjunctival haemorrhage with a history of trauma should arouse high suspicion for eye globe rupture.** → *(that is obscured by hemorrhage → refer to ophthalmologust* **[T]** *)* *[sic — \"ophthalmologust\"]*\n\nThe stem gives both halves of the book's condition — **large** haemorrhage, **history of trauma** — and adds a third finding the book does not print. So **A**.\n\n**The low IOP is the confirming sign and it comes from outside the chapter.** A ruptured globe leaks aqueous or vitreous, so the eye deflates and the digitally estimated pressure falls; a hypotonous eye after blunt trauma is a rupture until proved otherwise. *(**Not taken from the course material** — `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.6 prints no IOP finding for globe rupture, and gives the sign only as \"a large area of subconjunctival haemorrhage with a history of trauma\".)* ⚠️ **Digital tonometry on a suspected ruptured globe is itself dangerous** — pressing on a perforated eye can express its contents — so in practice the finding is noted and not sought. *(Also **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n| Option | What it is | Why the stem excludes it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Globe rupture** | Full-thickness breach of the corneoscleral coat | Large subconjunctival haemorrhage + trauma + **low IOP**. The key |\n| **B. Retrobulbar hemorrhage** | Bleeding into the orbit behind the globe | Gives **proptosis, a tense orbit and a RAISED** intraocular and orbital pressure — the opposite pressure finding, and it is an orbital emergency threatening the optic nerve |\n| **C. Hyphema** | Blood layered **in the anterior chamber** | A different compartment, and it is visible as a fluid level behind the cornea rather than under the conjunctiva. IOP after hyphema is typically **normal or raised** |\n| **D. Iridodialysis** | Disinsertion of the iris root from the ciliary body | A blunt-trauma iris injury seen as a D-shaped pupil and a dark peripheral crescent; it does not by itself lower IOP or cause a large subconjunctival haemorrhage |\n\n**B is the option to argue against explicitly, because it shares the whole history.** Retrobulbar haemorrhage also follows severe trauma and also fills the eye with blood — but the pressure moves the other way. **The stem's one measured number is what separates A from B, and it points down.** *(The proptosis and raised pressure of retrobulbar haemorrhage are **not taken from the course material**; chapter 6 does not cover it.)*\n\n**Why the haemorrhage is described as *large*.** The book's rule is about area, not presence: small subconjunctival haemorrhages are idiopathic, Valsalva-related or hypertensive, and it prints \"Assurance of the patient if there are no other ocular findings\" as the first line of management. A haemorrhage big enough to obscure the sclera is a haemorrhage big enough to **hide a scleral wound** — which is what the book's parenthetical says: \"that is obscured by hemorrhage → refer\".\n\n⚠️ **Contrast with `opmcq-c6-15` and note that neither key is wrong.** That question asks the mandatory step in *any* subconjunctival haemorrhage and keys **blood pressure**; this one gives trauma and hypotony and keys rupture. **The traumatic and the spontaneous haemorrhage are two different clinical problems sharing one appearance**, and the bank tests both.\n\n⚠️ **The lecture gives subconjunctival haemorrhage one slide** — `L6) Conjunctiva.txt` slide 65, four photographs, no text — and prints no cause, no management and no trauma warning.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-283` — \"A patient who sustained **severe head trauma** presented with a **large triangular subconjunctival hemorrhage coming from the fornix**. The next investigation is:\" — is the closest match in the corpus: same mechanism, same sign, but it asks for the **investigation** rather than the diagnosis. `opqb-t6-248` (\"A **subconjunctival hemorrhage**:\") pairs with `opmcq-c6-15` instead.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"A large subconjunctival haemorrhage after trauma should raise high suspicion of globe rupture because it can hide a scleral wound; a low IOP confirms it, where retrobulbar haemorrhage would raise the pressure instead.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.48 (Ch.6 Q27; the stem is printed "Digitally- measured IOP")' },

  { id:'opmcq-c6-28', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-conj',
    stem:"All the following are among the common causes of bacterial conjunctivitis except?",
    options:["H. influenzae","S. Pneumoniae","Moraxella catarrhalis","N. gonorrhea"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Two consecutive sentences on one book page answer this, and the bank has taken one option from each.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.91:\n\n> - The **most common organisms** are **H. influenzae, S. pneumoniae, S. aureus and Moraxella catarrhalis.**\n> - **Very few cases are caused by N. gonorrhoea** while **N. meningitidis** conjunctivitis is rare and usually affects children.\n\nOptions A, B and C are three of the four common organisms; option D is the first named organism of the **next** sentence, the one that begins \"Very few cases\". So **D**.\n\n| Option | The book's sentence | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. H. influenzae** | \"most common organisms\" | Common |\n| **B. S. Pneumoniae** | \"most common organisms\" | Common |\n| **C. Moraxella catarrhalis** | \"most common organisms\" | Common |\n| **D. N. gonorrhea** | \"**Very few cases** are caused by N. gonorrhoea\" | **Uncommon — the key** |\n\n⚠️ **The fourth common organism is missing from the menu: *S. aureus*.** The book names four and the bank offers three, replacing the fourth with the rare one. Worth noticing, because *S. aureus* is the organism the eyelid chapter builds staphylococcal blepharitis on.\n\n⚠️ **Rare does not mean unimportant, and gonococcal disease is the chapter's most dangerous organism.** It is referenced five times in chapter 6 and never given a section of its own:\n\n| Where | What the book says |\n|---|---|\n| p.85, discharge table | \"**Severe purulent discharge** is suggestive of **gonococcal** conjunctivitis\" |\n| p.85, membranes | Severe gonococcal infection causes a **pseudo-membrane** |\n| p.86, lymphadenopathy | \"Severe bacterial conjunctivitis (especially **gonococcal**)\" |\n| p.91, signs | \"**Corneal ulceration** in gonococcal and meningococcal infections\" |\n| p.91, treatment | \"**Systemic** antibiotics are needed in gonococcal and meningococcal and H influenzae infections\" |\n| p.91, investigations | \"Conjunctival swabs for urgent **gram staining** to exclude gonococcal and meningococcal infections\" |\n\n**So the exam's two gonococcal facts are: it is a rare cause, and it is the one that ulcerates the cornea and needs systemic treatment.** Those are not in tension — rarity and severity are different axes, and this question tests only the first.\n\n⚠️ **Two spelling notes, neither corrected.** The bank prints \"**S. Pneumoniae**\" with a capitalised species epithet where the book prints \"S. pneumoniae\", and \"**N. gonorrhea**\" where the book prints \"N. gonorrhoea\". Both are left as printed; neither changes the organism.\n\n⚠️ **No organism is taught in the conjunctiva lecture, measured.** `L6) Conjunctiva.txt`'s own register at line 1115 records `gonorrhea = 0 · Haemophil = 0 · Koch-Weeks = 0 · Morax / Moraxella = 0`, and line 1116 adds `Corynebact = 0 · diphther = 0 · \"Chlamydia trachomatis\" = 0 · adenovirus = 0`. Checked case-insensitively across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks: the only **live** Moraxella hit in the module is `L15,16) Cornea.txt` line 82, \"**Moraxella**: elderly, debilitated patients\" — and that is about Moraxella **keratitis**, a different disease in a different tissue. **This is a book answer with no lecture support at all.**\n\n⚠️ **This is the last question of chapter 6.** Its key prints on book p.48 and the rest of that page is blank; book p.49 opens the red banner \"7. The sclera\" with a fresh Q1.\n\n**Cross-bank:** `opqb-t6-235` (\"Organisms causing **angular conjunctivitis** are:\") is the nearest organism question and asks about *Moraxella lacunata*, which chapter 6 does not cover at all — its not-covered register records angular conjunctivitis as absent. `opqb-t6-243` (\"**Staphylococcal** infections in ophthalmology do **NOT** include:\") is the other organism-exclusion question and works a different list; `opqb-t6-265` (\"In **ophthalmia neonatorum** the most common **dangerous** causative organism is:\") is where gonococcus is keyed as an answer rather than excluded.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"The common causes of acute bacterial conjunctivitis are H. influenzae, S. pneumoniae, S. aureus and Moraxella catarrhalis; N. gonorrhoea causes very few cases but is the one that ulcerates the cornea and needs systemic antibiotics.",
    source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.48 (Ch.6 Q28; option B is printed "S. Pneumoniae" and option D "N. gonorrhea", where ophthalmology.pdf p.91 prints "S. pneumoniae" and "N. gonorrhoea")' }